===Etymology===
From [[w:Pantechnicon van|Pantechnicon]], a 19th-century firm which owned a building with a [[facade]] of [[Doric order|Doric]] [[column]]s in Motcomb Street, {{w|Belgrave Square}}, London, UK, with a picture gallery, a furniture shop, a shop selling [[carriage]]s, and a [[warehouse]] for storing customers’ furniture and other items. The firm used large [[horse-drawn]] [[van]]s to collect and deliver their customers’ property, which came to be known as Pantechnicon vans.

The word was coined by the firm from {{m|en|pan-||''prefix meaning ‘[[all]]’''}} (from {{der|en|grc|πᾶν}}, neuter form of {{m|grc|πᾶς||all, [[every]]}}) + {{m|grc|τεχνικόν}}, neuter singular of {{m|grc|τεχνικός||[[technical]]}}.

